# DISCLOSURE — pi-cursor (@pixu1980/pi-cursor)

## Purpose

pi-cursor registers a `cursor` model provider in the pi coding agent. It lets a
user run Cursor's own agents from pi using the user's own Cursor API key.

## Credential handling

| Property | Guarantee |
| --- | --- |
| Where the key is read from | pi's credential store (`/login cursor` → `~/.pi/agent/auth.json`) or `CURSOR_API_KEY` |
| Where the key is written | **Nowhere by this extension.** pi owns the auth file. pi-cursor contains no filesystem write that touches a credential. |
| Where the key is sent | `api2.cursor.sh` and `api.cursor.com` only, as `Authorization: Bearer …` |
| Before `/login` | `resolve()` hands pi a non-secret placeholder so the Cursor models stay visible in the picker. The stream path normalises it away, so no request is ever issued with it (asserted by test). |
| Where the key is logged | Nowhere. Every string that can reach the transcript, an error message, or stderr passes through `scrubSecrets` first. `/cursor-key` prints a masked label, never the value. |
| Cached key material | None. The model catalog cache holds model metadata and a timestamp; unlike upstream `pi-cursor-sdk` it stores **no key fingerprint**. |
| The Cursor SDK's own auth file (`~/.cursor/sdk/auth.json`) | Never read, never written. pi is the single source of truth. |

## Outbound surface

pi-cursor ships **no HTTP client of its own**: no `fetch`, no `http`/`https`
request, no third-party networking package. This is enforced by a source-level
test that fails the build if any file under `lib/` or `index.ts` gains `fetch(`,
`http`/`https` `.request`, `net.connect`, `tls.connect`, `WebSocket`,
`EventSource`, `undici`, `axios`, or a `child_process` call, and by a test that
rejects any URL literal whose host is not in the allowlist.

Every request on the wire is issued by the Cursor SDK, from three call sites the
extension drives: `Cursor.models.list()` (model discovery), `Agent.create()`
(one local agent per pi session), and `agent.send()` (one run per turn).

| Host | Service | Payload |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `api2.cursor.sh` | `auth/exchange_user_api_key` | The API key, as the bearer credential |
| `api2.cursor.sh` | `agent_service_connect`, `chat`, tool executors | Your prompt, your working directory's files as the agent reads/edits them |
| `api2.cursor.sh` | `aiserver.v1.AnalyticsService.TrackEvents` / `BootstrapStatsig` | Anonymous SDK counters: `surface`, `sdk_version`, `os_platform`, `os_arch`, run/duration/error-code fields |
| `api.cursor.com` | `GET /v1/models` | Nothing but the bearer credential |

Both hosts are Cursor-owned. There is no third-party analytics or crash
reporting in the path.

### Notes on the Cursor SDK's own behaviour

Verified against `@cursor/sdk@1.0.27` by static analysis of the published
bundle:

- Its vendored Statsig client (`@statsig/js-client@3.31.0`) is constructed with
  `preventAllNetworkTraffic: true`, `disableLogging: true`,
  `disableStorage: true`, and is fed from Cursor's own `BootstrapStatsig` RPC.
  The `statsigapi.net`, `featureassets.org`, `api.statsigcdn.com` and
  `prodregistryv2.org` endpoints are compiled into the library but are **not**
  reachable in this configuration.
- It reports run counters to Cursor's backend with the API key as the bearer
  token. No prompt, file content, or credential is part of those payloads.
- Upstream `pi-cursor-sdk` calls `api.cursor.com/v1/agents/{id}/usage`
  separately. pi-cursor does not: it reads usage from the run it already has.

## Guards

1. **Backend-override refusal.** The SDK honours `CURSOR_BACKEND_URL`. If it is
   set to anything outside the allowlist, pi-cursor refuses to resolve the API
   key at all, so nothing is sent. `PI_CURSOR_ALLOW_BACKEND_OVERRIDE=1` is the
   explicit opt-out for self-hosted Cursor-compatible stacks.
2. **Offline mode is enforced, not assumed.** The Cursor SDK does not read pi's
   `--offline` / `PI_OFFLINE` flag, so pi-cursor reads it and skips the startup
   model fetch; `/cursor-models` refuses as well. Without this check an offline
   run would still send the API key to Cursor.
3. **Secret scrubbing.** Every model output delta, activity trace line, error
   message and diagnostic is scrubbed before it is emitted.
4. **Bounded activity trace.** Tool activity is summarised (command, path,
   pattern) and capped, so file bodies are not copied into the session log.

## Deliberate non-goals

| Not implemented | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Cloud agents (`cloud.env`, `autoCreatePR`, …) | Uploads your repository to Cursor's cloud. Local agents only. |
| MCP tool bridge over loopback HTTP | Exposes pi's tools to the agent through a local server. Not needed: the Cursor agent has its own tools. |
| Usage reporting request | Redundant; the run already reports usage. |
| Custom telemetry, crash reporting, analytics | None. |
| `~/.cursor/sdk/auth.json` | pi owns credentials; a second copy is an unnecessary secret at rest. |

## Consequences the user should know

- The Cursor agent executes shell commands and edits files **itself**. pi's tool
  approval layer does not intercept them; the agent runs with pi's process
  permissions.
- Cursor's SDK reports anonymous run counters to Cursor's backend. This cannot be
  disabled from the client. It is Cursor-bound, carries no user content, and is
  disclosed here rather than hidden.
- pi-cursor adds no guarantee about what Cursor does with the data sent to it;
  that is governed by Cursor's own terms.
